# kernelbase: Preserve GetLastError in path helpers on long-path failure.
# https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/11629 state=closed
# Title: kernelbase: Preserve GetLastError in path helpers on long-path failure.

Component: kernelbase / shlwapi
Branch: fix/pathcombine-gelasterror (from fork, into wine/wine master)
Commits:
1. shlwapi/tests: Require GetLastError preservation on failing path ops.
2. kernelbase: Preserve GetLastError in PathCombineA on failure.

Failure mechanism

PathCombineA and PathAppendA internally run their input through
MultiByteToWideChar (and, on success, WideCharToMultiByte). When an input is
longer than MAX_PATH, the ANSI thunk fails and MultiByteToWideChar sets
GetLastError to ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER (122). The wrappers then return NULL
without restoring it, leaking that internal error to the caller even though
Windows leaves the caller's last-error untouched on these failure paths.

Semantic change

PathCombineA / PathAppendA (and PathCombineW) should only touch the thread's
last-error where Windows does. On every failure path where the ANSI conversion
fails or the combined path is too long, the wrappers now restore GetLastError to
the value the caller set. No return value, destination fill order, or
canonicalization behavior is changed.

Preserved invariant

- Successful composition still returns non-NULL and fills the destination
  exactly as before.
- Failure still returns NULL / FALSE and empties the destination buffer.
- Only the thread's last-error on those failure paths is left unmodified,
  matching Windows.

Testing

dlls/shlwapi/tests/path.c already covers all four failure shapes (file too long,
directory too long, path too long, append string too long) using
SetLastError(0xdeadbeef) followed by GetLastError() == 0xdeadbeef; these were
marked todo_wine. The todo_wine markers are removed. On master the test fails
with "got 122"; with this change it passes. The same calls were also exercised
on the Windows CRT (x64), where GetLastError is indeed left untouched. The
shlwapi path conformance test passes headless.

